    <!-- FAQ -->
    <div class="faq-section">
      <h2 class="faq-section-title" id="faqs">#7. Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <div class="faq-item" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
        <button class="faq-question">
          What is the difference between redaction and metadata removal?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <div class="faq-answer-inner">Redaction removes visible content from the page — names, numbers, images drawn over with black boxes. Metadata removal strips the hidden data embedded in the file itself: who created it, when, on what software, and revision history. They target completely separate layers. Neither one does the other's job.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
        <button class="faq-question">
          Does redacting a PDF also remove metadata?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <div class="faq-answer-inner">No. Redaction only affects the visible content layer. The metadata — Author, Creator, modification timestamps, Keywords — stays intact regardless of how much content you redact. You need a separate metadata removal step after redaction.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
        <button class="faq-question">
          Can you read redacted text from PDF metadata?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <div class="faq-answer-inner">Not from standard metadata fields, no. But if redaction was applied incorrectly — a black rectangle drawn on top of text rather than true redaction — the underlying text can be extracted via copy-paste or a text extraction tool. That's a redaction failure, not a metadata issue. They're separate problems.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
        <button class="faq-question">
          When do you need both redaction and metadata removal?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <div class="faq-answer-inner">Any time you share a document externally and it contains sensitive visible content. Legal filings, FOIA responses, employment records, client reports with confidential sections — all need both. If the document has no sensitive visible content but you still don't want your author name and internal tags exposed, you need metadata removal only.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
        <button class="faq-question">
          What happens if you use a black box to redact instead of a proper redaction tool?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <div class="faq-answer-inner">The text under the black box remains in the file. Anyone who selects the area and copies, or runs the PDF through a text extraction tool, can read the "redacted" content. This has caused documented legal, political, and national security incidents. Always use a tool that permanently removes underlying text — not one that covers it.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
        <button class="faq-question">
          Is metadata removal required under GDPR?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <div class="faq-answer-inner">GDPR Article 4(1) defines personal data as anything that identifies or can identify a natural person. An author's full name in a metadata field qualifies. The data minimisation principle (Article 5(1)(c)) requires that personal data be "adequate, relevant and limited to what is necessary." Metadata that serves no purpose in the distributed document should be stripped. Failing to do so may be a compliance issue depending on context.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

